{{smaller block|If it should at any time so happen that these rights should be invaded, there is no remedy but a reliance on the courts to protect and ''vindicate'' them.
{{right|''D. Webster'', Remarks in Convention to Revise Const., 1821.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
3. To support or maintain as true or correct, against denial, censure, or objections; defend; justify.
{{smaller block|<poem>Laugh where we must, be candid where we can;
But ''vindicate'' the ways of God to man.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, i. 16.}}}}
{{smaller block|We can only ''vindicate'' the fidelity of Sallust at the expense of his skill. {{right|''Macaulay'', History.}}}}
'''4†.''' To avenge; punish; retaliate.
{{smaller block|<poem>The senate
And people of Rome, of their accustomed greatness,
Will sharply and severely ''vindicate'',
Not only any fact, but any practice
Or purpose ’gainst the state. ''B. Jonson'', Catiline, iv. 4.</poem>}}
='''Syn. 2''' and '''3.''' ''Assert, Defend, Maintain'', etc. See ''assert.''

'''vindicate†''' (vin′di-kāt), ''a.'' Vindicated.
{{smaller block|He makes Velleius highly ''vindicate'' from this imputation. {{right|''J. Howe'', Works, i. 2.}}}}

'''vindication''' (vin-di-kā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''vindicatio(n-)'', a claiming, a defense, < ''vindicare'', claim: see ''vindicate.''] The act of vindicating, or the state of being vindicated. {{smaller|(''a'') A justification against denial or censure, or against objections or accusations.}}
{{smaller block|This is no ''vindication'' of her conduct.
{{right|''Broome'', Notes on the Odyssey, iv. 375.}}}}
{{smaller block|It was now far too late in Clifford’s life for the good opinion of society to be worth the trouble and anguish of a formal ''vindication''. {{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, xxi.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The act of supporting by proof or legal process; the proving of anything to be true or just: as, the ''vindication'' of a title, claim, or right. (''c'') Defense from wrong or oppression, by force or otherwise; maintenance of a cause against an assailant or enemy: as, the ''vindication'' of the rights of man; the ''vindication'' of liberties.}}
{{smaller block|If one proud man injure or oppress an humble man, it is a thousand to one another undertakes his patronage, defence, and ''vindication''. {{right|''Sir M. Hale'', Humility.}}}}
